WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.730
- We think the cyber threat

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:04.769
is increasing almost exponentially.

00:00:04.769 --> 00:00:07.240
- Ransomware is becoming
an increasingly severe

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.430
and pervasive problem.

00:00:08.430 --> 00:00:09.980
- We all have a role to play

00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.000
in keeping our nation safe.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.880
No organization is immune.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.510
- We always thought we
were small potatoes.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:17.940
Who would pick a single library out

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:20.740
of the world to attack?

00:00:20.740 --> 00:00:21.730
And that just goes to show

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:23.330
that it could happen to anybody.

00:00:24.831 --> 00:00:26.230
- [Narrator] Ransomware
is a practice hackers use

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:27.530
to lock up computer systems

00:00:27.530 --> 00:00:29.150
and demand a payment from victims

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:30.650
for their release.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.650
Data on ransomware attacks varies widely

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:33.920
and estimates are difficult to make

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.760
because many attacks are never made public

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:37.710
or reported to law enforcement.

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:39.070
But security officials agree,

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.600
the problem is rapidly getting worse.

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.620
These attacks are increasing in frequency,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.280
victim losses are skyrocketing

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.720
and hackers are shifting their targets

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:48.220
away from data-rich companies

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:49.940
to providers of key public needs,

00:00:49.940 --> 00:00:52.393
such as hospitals,
transportation and food.

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:55.610
What's causing the current
acceleration in frequency

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.580
and scope of these attacks?

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.010
There are at least four main factors.

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:03.240
First, victims continue to
make cybersecurity mistakes,

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.840
like clicking on phishing links,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.860
or failing to keep
company software updated.

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:09.680
- These criminal groups are
still finding it too easy

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.676
to exploit weak cybersecurity

00:01:11.676 --> 00:01:15.780
or failure to execute
cybersecurity best practices.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:17.760
- [Narrator] A ransomware
attack on Colonial Pipeline

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.290
forced a 5,500-mile pipeline
to shut down for six days,

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:24.470
spurring a run on gasoline
along parts of the East Coast.

00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:26.010
Investigators believe hackers were able

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.900
to break into the
company's computer systems

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.480
by logging into an out-of-use VPN profile

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.380
that lacked two-factor authentication.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:37.110
The public library in Brownsburg, Indiana

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:40.620
found themselves in a
similar situation in 2017.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:43.190
- We believe the hackers came in

00:01:43.190 --> 00:01:45.505
through our card catalog,

00:01:45.505 --> 00:01:48.280
which is where you look for books.

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:49.360
It's open to the public,

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:50.783
so it's an open venue.

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:52.800
- [Narrator] The attack locked Robinson

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:54.380
and her staff out of email,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:57.210
card holder information, and
their entire library catalog

00:01:57.210 --> 00:01:59.320
of over 100,000 items.

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.110
The library paid half of a Bitcoin,

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:03.230
approximately $1,300 at the time,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:04.313
to regain access.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.610
Second, ransomware attacks

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:10.020
have become very lucrative for hackers.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.310
In the past several weeks alone,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:13.030
JBS, whose meat plants

00:02:13.030 --> 00:02:15.120
were temporarily knocked
offline by cyber criminals

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:17.910
and Colonial Pipeline
have paid many millions

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.650
of dollars to get their
systems back up and running.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.260
- Considering the consequences

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:24.440
of potentially not bringing
the pipeline back on

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:26.690
as quickly as I possibly could.

00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:29.510
I chose the option to
make the ransom payment.

00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:30.650
- [Narrator] Both the Colonial Pipeline

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.430
and JBS ransom payments were
made using cryptocurrency,

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.900
which has a reputation
for being hard to track.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:37.430
But an announcement by
the Justice Department

00:02:37.430 --> 00:02:39.180
that they'd recovered about 2.3 million

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:40.720
of Colonial's initial ransom

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:42.700
demonstrates investigators'
growing technical ability

00:02:42.700 --> 00:02:45.390
in some cases to disrupt
the financial infrastructure

00:02:45.390 --> 00:02:46.800
that has enabled these ransomware gangs

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:48.390
to demand large sums.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.840
- The sophisticated use of technology

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:54.470
to hold businesses and
even whole cities hostage

00:02:54.470 --> 00:02:59.470
for profit is decidedly
a 21st century challenge.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:03.970
But the old adage follow
the money still applies.

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.440
- [Narrator] Cryptocurrency
isn't the only reason

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.640
ransomware attacks have
become more lucrative.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:10.530
Some hackers are now utilizing
double extortion tactics

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:11.870
in which they steal sensitive data

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:12.703
from their victims

00:03:12.703 --> 00:03:14.190
before encrypting it and then threaten

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:17.550
to publish that information
if victims refuse to pay.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.640
Third, hacking groups have
reinvented the process

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.770
through which criminal
networks extort victims.

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:22.886
Ransomware-as-a-service is a practice

00:03:22.886 --> 00:03:24.100
in which hacking networks

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.710
can sell or lease their
ransomware software

00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:28.000
to other criminal groups
to carry out attacks.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:28.970
Those affiliate groups

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.580
then share the proceeds with the hackers

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:31.830
who developed it.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.230
DarkSide, the criminal group linked

00:03:33.230 --> 00:03:36.090
to the Colonial Pipeline
hack works in this way.

00:03:36.090 --> 00:03:37.830
And finally, according to US officials

00:03:37.830 --> 00:03:39.290
and cybersecurity researchers,

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:40.300
the majority of these hacks

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.240
are coming from Russia and Eastern Europe

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.430
where security experts say the line

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:45.770
between criminal hacking groups

00:03:45.770 --> 00:03:48.170
and state-backed cyber
operations can be murky

00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:50.350
as governments often
tolerate criminal activity

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:52.240
as long as it is targeted overseas

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.680
and sometimes even recruit hackers

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:56.280
from those groups to carry
out their own objectives.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.720
Following a highly anticipated summit

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:01.100
between President Biden and
Vladimir Putin in Geneva,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:02.280
the Russian leader said that he agreed

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:03.989
to start consultations on cybersecurity

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:05.840
and denied that Moscow was involved

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:07.380
in any cyber attacks.

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:08.500
At a separate news conference,

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:10.420
President Biden said
he presented Mr. Putin

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:12.090
with a list of critical infrastructure

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:14.050
that he said should be off limits.

00:04:14.050 --> 00:04:16.170
- Responsible countries
need to take action

00:04:16.170 --> 00:04:19.550
against criminals who
conduct ransomware activities

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:21.630
on their territory.

00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:24.940
So we agreed to task experts

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:26.553
in both our countries

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.559
to work on specific understandings

00:04:29.559 --> 00:04:32.070
about what's off limits

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.800
and to follow up on specific cases

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.870
that originate in other countries,

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:39.080
in either of our countries.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.390
- [Narrator] So what can
individuals and companies

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:42.400
do now to better protect themselves?

00:04:42.400 --> 00:04:43.850
Goldstein said CISA is focused

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.420
on helping ensure that
organizations understand basic steps

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:48.250
that they need to adopt
to protect themselves.

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:49.780
Like running up-to-date software

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.950
and using multi-factor authentication.

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:53.590
He hopes the intrusions
at Colonial Pipeline

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:55.090
and JBS serve as a wake-up call

00:04:55.090 --> 00:04:57.490
for organizations across the country.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.550
- The first barrier to increasing adoption

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.870
of cybersecurity best practices

00:05:02.870 --> 00:05:05.700
is ensuring awareness
among business management

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:07.140
and business leaders

00:05:07.140 --> 00:05:08.830
to make sure that when companies

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.960
are deciding where to invest

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:13.530
and which risks to invest in driving down,

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.440
cybersecurity and the
risk of ransomware attacks

00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:18.911
is seen as a top-priority risk.

00:05:18.911 --> 00:05:20.780
- [Narrator] Robinson said
that after they recovered

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:22.410
from the ransomware attack on the library,

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:23.790
they implemented many security changes

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.020
to prevent future attacks,

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:26.960
like reworking their server backup system.

00:05:26.960 --> 00:05:31.310
- We made sure that our
catalog was on a firewall.

00:05:31.310 --> 00:05:33.620
We have a product called
Silence that's running

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:37.650
that's watching for not just known viruses

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.600
but unusual activity.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.433
- [Narrator] Following
the ransomware attack

00:05:40.433 --> 00:05:41.690
on Colonial Pipeline,

00:05:41.690 --> 00:05:42.890
President Biden said ransomware

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:45.761
was a growing problem in
need of a global response.

00:05:45.761 --> 00:05:47.320
As part of that response,

00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:48.750
TSA released security directives

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:49.840
requiring pipeline operators

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:50.940
to provide notification

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.270
when they are targets or
victims of cyber attacks,

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:55.160
to appoint a cybersecurity coordinator,

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:56.960
and to review their current activities.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.040
And some lawmakers and former
government officials say

00:05:59.040 --> 00:05:59.900
that the federal government

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:02.250
should now expand mandatory
cybersecurity requirements

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.160
across critical infrastructure sectors.

00:06:04.160 --> 00:06:06.360
Additionally, some lawmakers
have said they may be open

00:06:06.360 --> 00:06:07.550
to considering legislation

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:09.970
that could make ransom
payments illegal in some cases

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:11.610
or require companies to disclose

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:14.120
when they make a ransom
payment to hackers.

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.490
FBI officials said the
techniques they used

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:16.930
to recover some of Colonial funds

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:18.580
can be used in future cases,

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:19.680
including when hackers attempt

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:20.690
to transfer cryptocurrency

00:06:20.690 --> 00:06:22.570
through unfriendly overseas jurisdictions.

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:25.100
- We are looking at this as a whole

00:06:25.100 --> 00:06:26.160
of solutions effort

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.880
in which we need to focus
on the groups themselves,

00:06:29.880 --> 00:06:32.610
their infrastructures, and
then shoring up defenses

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:34.070
across victim companies

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.720
and by focusing deeply
on each of those areas,

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:39.713
we'll be able to drive
down this risk over time.

00:06:39.713 --> 00:06:42.463
(dramatic music)

